  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Aarakocra did a lot of things first. They’re the first entry in the Monster\n\tManual. They’re the first playable race in 5e that could be considered an\n\tanthromorph (an animal person). They’re the first playable race with flight.\n\tThey’re the first race banned from Adventurer’s League. All exciting stuff."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mechanically, Aarakocra are frustratingly bland. They get ability score increases, flight, talons, and a disappointing innate spell that you’ll rarely use. They’re a flying stat block. You play an Aarakocra either because you want to be a flying bird person or because you want flight at first level and don’t particularly care where it comes from."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Despite this blandness, Aarakocra are extremely powerful for a variety of builds, even without the custom origin rules or the updated racial traits. Dexterity and Wisdom is an excellent combination of ability score increases, and racial flight is insanely powerful, especially at a whopping 50 ft. of fly speed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Before we look at class options, I want to take a moment to explain exactly why flight is such a big deal. In many encounters, it’s a guaranteed win condition. Armed with a ranged weapon and sufficient ammunition (or a good cantrip like Eldritch Blast), a flying humanoid can eventually kill anything that can’t find a way to get full cover. The flyer can easily chase enemies around above ground and wear them down while safely well out of the defender’s range unless the defender can also fly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Even in enclosed spaces like a dungeon, a high ceiling can easily put the flying creature well out of their enemies’ reach. Most creatures in the monster manual lack a method to counter the simple issue of an enemy being 5 feet too high for the monster to attack them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-6",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "To summarize: Aarakocra can fly directly above most of the monster manual and kill the monster regardless of CR solely because you can fly and most monsters can’t."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-7",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you’re using the updated version of the Aarakocra published in Monsters of the Multiverse, you should understand how Gust of Wind works. It makes it difficult for enemies to approach you, but remember that it has an Action casting time and doesn’t do anything when you cast it except push creatures in the initial area of effect."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
